Z||000002||Return of Fiero||10-26-2001||01:28 AM||||they dont have the info i need. it has the colorsand the posts, no gauge, but a general "this goes here" but nothing substantial.for example, it has a fuseable link, with nothing to attach it to (going by the book) and soon enough, it will also have my hair on it as i rip it out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> i wish people would stop cutting wires when they "think" they know what they are doing. when i get 'em its, "here you go, good luck"<BR>"yeah, heres you salute, pal,"is what id like to say to those people, but im to civilized.<BR>anyhow, thank you for the advice  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> any input is good input, and you gave me more input that i expected to get. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Btw, did everyone change the wireing in the 84 cept me? seems like everyone decided they wanted to change 'em.<BR>Myke||||reg||1
Z||000003||William Federle||10-26-2001||08:38 AM||||I think that there is one big wire (from the battery) and two smaller ones on the big terminal. I think that there is one #10 wire that goes on the small terminal.<P>The wire going to the small terminal is about a #10 wire and it is the one that energizes the starter solenoid.<P>The two small wires on the big terminal are fusible links that are there to pick up 12 volts.<P>I might be wrong, but I think that all of the above wires are red. I guess you'd have to trace the solenoid-activating small red wire back to some location that shows up in the Haynes manual.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 10-26-2001).]||||reg||1
